Without implementing a lag-day logic, the reconciliation evaluates all records immediately. As a result, some recent records may initially appear as missing if they have not yet reached the downstream flow. In subsequent reconciliation runs, previously unmatched records are checked again and updated if a matching record is found. This means the latest day's reconciliation may temporarily show a lower match rate, but it improves automatically as late-arriving records are processed.


From a reconciliation perspective, the impact is mainly on how we interpret the results, not on the matching itself.

Our reconciliation process searches for a matching record within a ±15-day window and stores the actual time difference (diff_days, diff_hours, diff_minutes, abs_diff_hours). So the reconciliation engine itself is not dependent on a lag-day rule.

Without implementing a lag-day wait:

A record that has not yet arrived in the downstream flow at the time of today's run will initially appear as MISSING_IN_FLOW.
If that same record arrives later (for example, 6 or 12 hours later), the next day's reconciliation run will find it and update the status to MATCHED_IN_FLOW, along with the actual time difference.

Therefore, the reconciliation result becomes eventually consistent over successive runs.

The only impact is that the dashboard for the most recent day may temporarily show a slightly lower reconciliation percentage until late-arriving records are picked up in the next execution.

Since we already capture the actual arrival difference (abs_diff_hours), we can calculate SLA compliance directly in the reporting layer, for example:

matched within 24 hours
matched after 24 hours
matched after 48 hours

without adding additional complexity to the reconciliation logic itself.

Example
Run Date	CSV Record	Flow Arrival	Initial Result	Next Run Result
10 Feb	9 Feb 23:50	Not arrived yet	MISSING_IN_FLOW	—
11 Feb	Same record	Arrived at 10 Feb 08:15	—	MATCHED_IN_FLOW (abs_diff_hours = 8.4)

So, although the 10 Feb dashboard temporarily shows one missing record, the 11 Feb run automatically corrects it.

Trade-off

Advantages of not using lag-day logic

Simpler reconciliation process.
No special waiting or exclusion rules.
All SLA calculations can be derived from abs_diff_hours.
Easier to maintain and explain.

Consideration

The latest day's dashboard may show a small number of temporary misses until the next reconciliation run. These are corrected automatically on subsequent runs.

For this reason, I think the simpler design is appropriate, provided stakeholders understand that the most recent day's reconciliation is provisional and will stabilize after the next scheduled run.
